  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of sulfur redox chemistry inside a porous carbon host, likely illustrating the discharge pathway in a lithium–sulfur battery. Yellow regions mark sulfur, embedded within a blue carbon framework. At the left, solid sulfur appears to dissolve into a soluble form, and then a series of sulfur species with decreasing chain length are shown in the electrolyte-filled region. Arrows connect these intermediates, indicating stepwise conversion among polysulfides, including longer-chain species that transform into shorter ones.\n\nAs the process moves toward the right, the sulfur species become progressively reduced and react with incoming lithium ions from the lower region. Dashed arrows suggest Li+ transport toward the reaction zone. Near the carbon surface on the right, the final discharge products, labeled Li2S2 and Li2S, appear to precipitate or deposit locally.\n\nOverall, the image conveys the central mechanism of lithium–sulfur conversion: sulfur dissolves and cycles through multiple soluble polysulfide intermediates before ending as insoluble lithium sulfides. The porous carbon seems to provide the conductive matrix and confinement environment where these transformations occur.",
